@*
    Task 7.3 — the ASP.NET Core replacement for Views/Web.config's
    <system.web.webPages.razor><pages> element (Requirement 4.4).

    Views/Web.config declared two things that every view in this folder tree depended on:

      pageBaseType="Nop.Web.Framework.ViewEngines.Razor.WebViewPage"
          -> the @inherits directive below. This is what makes T("Some.Resource") resolve:
             the localizer lives on that base class. Without it every view fails with
             ~2000 CS0103 "The name 'T' does not exist in the current context" errors.

      <namespaces>          -> the @using directives below.

    Views/Web.config itself is NOT deleted here — task 7.5 owns removing obsolete files.
    It is inert either way: the Web SDK copies it as content, and nothing reads
    system.web.webPages.razor on .NET 10.
*@
@inherits Nop.Web.Framework.ViewEngines.Razor.WebViewPage<TModel>

@*
    Direct translations of the four System.Web entries in Views/Web.config:
        System.Web.Mvc       -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc
        System.Web.Mvc.Html  -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Rendering (IHtmlHelper extensions)
        System.Web.Routing   -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing
        System.Web.Mvc.Ajax  -> no counterpart; the Ajax.* helpers do not exist in
                                ASP.NET Core and no view in this project used them
                                (verified: zero "Ajax." occurrences under Views/).
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html is added for IHtmlContent/HtmlString, which replace
    MvcHtmlString throughout.
*@
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Rendering
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing

@* carried over verbatim from Views/Web.config *@
@using Nop.Web.Extensions
@using Nop.Web.Framework
@using Nop.Web.Framework.Security.Captcha
@using Nop.Web.Framework.Security.Honeypot
@using Nop.Web.Framework.UI

@* used by nearly every view for the localization/SEO/model helpers *@
@using Nop.Core
@using Nop.Core.Domain.Catalog
@using Nop.Core.Domain.Common
@using Nop.Core.Domain.Customers
@using Nop.Core.Domain.Orders
@using Nop.Core.Infrastructure
@using Nop.Services.Localization
@using Nop.Services.Seo
@using Nop.Web.Models.Blogs
@*
    Nop.Web.Models.Boards and Nop.Web.Models.Catalog are deliberately NOT imported globally:
    both declare a SearchModel, so a global import makes the name ambiguous in
    Views/Boards/Search.cshtml and Views/Catalog/Search.cshtml. Views in those areas already
    reference their models by full name in @model, and the handful that need more declare
    their own @using.
*@
@using Nop.Web.Models.Checkout
@using Nop.Web.Models.Cms
@using Nop.Web.Models.Common
@using Nop.Web.Models.Customer
@using Nop.Web.Models.Media
@using Nop.Web.Models.News
@using Nop.Web.Models.Newsletter
@using Nop.Web.Models.Order
@using Nop.Web.Models.Polls
@using Nop.Web.Models.PrivateMessages
@using Nop.Web.Models.Profile
@using Nop.Web.Models.ShoppingCart
@using Nop.Web.Models.Topics
@using Nop.Web.Models.Vendors

@*
    Tag helpers. 3.90 had none (they do not exist in MVC 5), so nothing here is a port —
    these are added because the framework's own form/validation/link tag helpers are the
    supported way to get anti-forgery tokens emitted into forms, and Html.BeginForm relies
    on the same infrastructure.
*@
@addTagHelper *, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers
